r Imaging Centre, Royal Marsden Hospital and the Institute of Cancer Research, Downs Road, Sutton, Surrey, SM2 5PT, UK."}]}}, {"fullName": "Payne GS", "lastName": "Payne", "firstName": "Geoffrey S", "initials": "GS"}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-8993-5301"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "22", "volume": "60", "journalIssueId": 2339169, "dateOfPublication": "2015 Nov", "monthOfPublication": 11, "yearOfPublication": 2015, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Phys Med Biol", "title": "Physics in medicine and biology", "essn": "1361-6560", "issn": "0031-9155", "nlmid": "0401220", "isoabbreviation": "Phys Med Biol"}, "printPublicationDate": "2015-11-01"}, "pubYear": "2015", "pageInfo": "R323-61", "abstractText": "The use of magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) in radiotherapy (RT) planning is rapidly expanding. We review the wide range of image contrast mechanisms available to MRI and the way they are exploited for RT planning. However a number of challenges are also considered: the requirements that MR images are acquired in the RT treatment position, that they are geometrically accurate, that effects of patient motion during the scan are minimized, that tissue markers are clearly demonstrated, that an estimate of electron density can be obtained. These issues are discussed in detail, prior to the consideration of a number of specific clinical applications. This is followed by a brief discussion on the development of real-time MRI-guided RT.", "affiliation": "Cancer Research UK Cancer Imaging Centre, Royal Marsden Hospital and the Institute of Cancer Research, Downs Road, Sutton, Surrey, SM2 5PT, UK.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print-Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "research-article", "Journal Article"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "National Institute for Health Research (